{
  "dom_id": "step-3664",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "**Periodic functions** are special functions that repeat themselves every ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/b332ba26d1b8a3e4b8f817ed7eed0f807c5cc8457a0f29182ba04d25cbcb41b7.svg) units. The smallest positive value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/b332ba26d1b8a3e4b8f817ed7eed0f807c5cc8457a0f29182ba04d25cbcb41b7.svg) that has this property is called the **period** of the function. \n\nLet's take the following periodic function as an example:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/c8012ef5d26d04c81a6200bffb85babe8787c49258f5c014e6ac42ccbbbbf3cb.png)\n\nThis function repeats itself with a period of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) To illustrate, first focus on the part of the graph shown in blue below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/c309cafb184c76a6630f4357e07267ad832e85dd982fffce3abd28bf1526ec95.png)\n\nIf we shift this part ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the right, the graph remains unchanged!\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/a82b5b1fd4da072d954d19bae0a790e82704343ec8b9d4448076f9daebe90617.png)\n\nThe same would happen if we shifted the part ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the left. \n\nBy knowing the period of a periodic function, we can use a value of the function to infer many other values of the function. For example, in the function above, note that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/4ba0fb2ba36b90b8993c542124873b19eb240bed743d3c7bce2712f043187c4e.svg) Since the period is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/0be337b1b7891cff68331fbd1be3c8eb739eb8f47e7788e487ab91a8b2c88b67.svg) if we add (or subtract) any multiple of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) from the input, the output stays the same. We can write this as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/b3d30eb46010a29136217b3d004e0878a8923a94e77a52192172638697b6c67a.svg)\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is an integer. \n\nFor example, setting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/a44568f6fc5aa5bd466f76f6220d308431c1187631cf36f8bebf2290ef7a0d83.svg) gives the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/1e73284bd11385d40e11887964fa4ad1a7f523982c885c5fca7f87197ea1c954.svg)\n\nThe same is true for negative values of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg) Setting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/55af3f37f9d82f0e1d9e6a77096eba247f13ea48ea005c2fd0b8719f1d6c04c1.svg) gives the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14230523/assets/7df85879c234dbc914f4f4ee9261f4e12f0a57b03ae7ab433ddd0c8c4ba14d7f.svg)",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
